**We are looking for an Engine Room Officer to join our fleet in Brazil, which is in full expansion.**

You will have the opportunity to work on a fleet of modern vessels with high technological capacity, and take part in dynamic and highly complex operations, such as:

- **Handling of anchors and deployment of torpedo anchors** on **AHTS** vessels;
- **Inspection, maintenance, subsea construction and saturation diving** on **RSV, CSV, DSV and MPSV** vessels;
- **Deployment of flexible lines** on **PLSV** vessels.

We are looking for professionals who bring **expertise in operating and maintaining the engine room**, combined with strong process management skills, with a focus on safety, operational efficiency and technical excellence.

**Location:**

This is a position that will be carried out offshore on board one of the vessels in our fleet on a 28x28 rotation.

**Who we are:**

DOF is one of the leading providers of integrated marine and subsea services for the global offshore energy market. We combine expertise, assets and technology to carry out integrated subsea and offshore operations safely and reliably — always.

With a multinational team of approximately 6,000 people and the largest global fleet of Construction Support Vessels (CSVs) and high-standard Anchor Handling Tug Supply vessels (AHTS), we support our partners with global reach, scale and industry-leading capability. Our operational centres and business units are located in Norway, Denmark, the United Kingdom, the USA, Singapore, Brazil, Argentina, Canada, Angola and Australia.

At DOF, you join a trusted global organisation where safety, teamwork and integrity come first. We offer career growth, continuous development and a culture that values inclusion, recognition and connection.

We value the right person for the role based on genuine alignment with our culture and purpose. We know that many people do not apply if they do not meet 100% of the requirements — and we want to change that.

Building a diverse, inclusive and positive environment is a priority for us. This position is also open to people with disabilities (PwD).

Diversity expands our access to the best talent — there are no limits for those who have a passion for what they do.

**Below are some of the duties of the position:**

Tasks include, but are not limited to:

- Carrying out watch duties and other orders described in the "Standing Orders of the Chief Engineer";
- Replacing the Second Engineer in his absence or incapacity;
- Recording watch occurrences in the Engine Logbook;
- Undertaking repair work that can be done with onboard resources, in addition to the maintenance and adjustment of various equipment;
- Operating and ensuring the proper functioning of the engines, electrical system, refrigeration, pumps, steering gear, cargo handling equipment, fuel oil and lubricating oil systems, and others as instructed by your superior;
- Carrying out planned and routine maintenance on equipment under your responsibility;
- Taking part in the vessel's bunkering when on duty;
- Monitoring compliance with legal requirements within your area of work;
- In the absence or incapacity of the Safety Officer on board, ensuring that safety procedures are carried out within your area of work, such as Permit to Work, Toolbox Talks, JSA, etc. To do so, request assistance from the Chief Engineer / Second Engineer whenever necessary;
- Maintaining the vessel's ability to withstand heavy seas;
- Operating all internal communication systems;
- Being familiar with emergency procedures and with switching control from remote to local and vice versa;
- Keeping the installations, equipment and utensils entrusted to you in good operating, organised and clean condition;
- When designated by your superior, familiarising and instructing Engine Cadets and Engine Trainees following the checklist they present;
- Checking that your "Seafarer's Health Certificate" is valid and ensuring it is renewed before it expires;
- Taking part in integrated fainas (vessel operations);
- Taking precautions and contributing to the prevention of marine environmental pollution;
- Working carefully and skilfully, using the necessary PPE to perform your duties safely and requiring others involved to use it;
- Knowing your function and the others described in the vessel's emergency duty master table;
- Learning about the distribution and operation of the vessel's safety and lifesaving equipment;
- Taking part in risk assessments before activities.

**What you must bring:**

- Completed higher education - STCW III/1;
- Valid certificates for the position;
- Experience in the role;
- Desirable: experience on anchor handling vessels and/or subsea operations;
- Advanced English (will be tested).

**You will stand out if you have:**

- Experience on AHTS and/or subsea vessels;
- Kongsberg DP Technician.

- Meal allowance;
- Health plan;
- Life insurance;
- Dental plan;
- Gympass;
- Relaxed and motivating environment;
- Respect for inclusion and diversity;
- And more.

